From 4b1eabeb4c3fc191c0ae51a060b77f243e03326e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 06:43:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] raster: narrow every fill to its clip and split large ones
 into bands
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Multithreading roadmap item #3 — the port of item #4's band partitioner and
item #5's clip narrowing into the second copy of the scanline rasterizer, the
one behind BImageRenderer and so behind Broiler.UI and the Writer.

BCanvas keeps a running intersection of its including clips, one entry per clip
operation, and every primitive clamps its pixel loop to it before drawing. The
clamp is exact rather than approximate: a pixel outside the box is one the
per-pixel IsVisible test already rejects, so the surviving pixels are
bit-identical and only the iterations go away. An excluding clip carries the
box forward unchanged, because it can only remove pixels from the admitted set
and a bound has to stay a superset of it. Layers record the box in force when
they were pushed, so compositing walks that box instead of the whole surface.

BRasterParallelism splits a large fill's scanlines across threads, budgeted by
BROILER_RASTER_THREADS (default one per core) — deliberately the same variable
the sibling copy reads, so a host dials down "the managed rasterizer" once. The
glyph path's coverage accumulator and crossing list move inside the band, which
is the only mutable state a scanline carries.

It also carries a floor the sibling does not have: a fill that can only be cut
two ways runs inline. A two-way split measured 437.7 ms against 362.9 ms for
the sequential path on the benchmark's largest scene, reproducing to a tenth of
a millisecond across separate processes, while three bands measured 297.9 and
four 270.4. Without the floor a two-core host would run slower with the
parallelism on than off.

Exit gate: 34 cases in RasterBandParallelismTests over every primitive that
goes through the partitioner, each rendered at budgets of 1, 2, 3, 4 and 8 and
compared byte for byte against its own single-threaded render; the clipped
renders are additionally compared against an unclipped render masked by the
clip, which is what would catch a narrowing that dropped a pixel the clip
admits. Suite 99 tests, 0 failures. Reverting the narrowing by one pixel fails
five of them, two of which predate this change.
